it's great to be joined today by dr.  Hagee Levine  who is from Hebrew University Hadassah  School of Public Health he's also the  lead author of a meta-analysis into what  is happening with sperm count and male  fertility  dr. Levine it's so great to talk to you  you know I've been reading more and more  about this issue and I think it's one  that a lot of people may not even know  is going on so let's introduce the  audience to what you've been studying  what trend are you seeing with in sperm  count and male fertility around the  world well if I may before I start I  actually start my presentation with the  famous statue of David and this is to  mention that we are interested in male  fertility not only of Kings for many  years however for some reason we totally  neglected male fertility now what's  important about the trend of sperm count  before we get to the results is that  sperm count is not only a great  indicator for male fertility it's also  influenced a lot by the environment so  we can learn from it about the impact of  the environment on human health and more  than that recent studies have shown that  sperm count and semen quality is a  predictor of morbidity and mortality so  if we have a problem with sperm count it  suggests that we have problem with men's  health in general so against this  background we wanted to know what are  the trends of sperm counts around the  world now 25 years ago there was a  seminal paper by Carlson and others  which claimed that there is a decline in  sperm count at least in Western  countries however this paper was  received with a lot of criticism and  there was still a debate so what we try  to achieve is to systematically review  the literature we screened over  7,500 papers to get all the papers with  good data on sperm count and to model  the trends over time this is called a  meta-analysis and what were the results  that you found when doing this  meta-analysis so the results were  shocking no matter how we analyze the  data there was over fifty percent  decline in both sperm concentration and  total sperm count among men from Western  countries over the last 40 years imagine  any indicator human indicator that  declined by 50 percent in 40 years when  we modelled only studies after 1995 the  trend was even steeper and this is after  controlling for various factors such as  age and abstinence time so if we you  know if we summarize it houston we have  a problem we have a problem with sperm  count among men from Western countries  unfortunately we simply don't have  enough data to know what are the current  trends among men from non-western  countries yeah so one question was going  to be what do we know about men from  non-western countries you've sort of  preempted that by saying we don't  exactly know can you talk a little bit  about within Western countries are there  any other trends whether they are  demographic trends whether they are  particular countries is it most  unnoticeable within any other subgroups  that you've looked at so I am a  scientist as a scientist I focus on the  facts and the fact is that in this study  our aim was to study the trends and I  cannot say based on this study what may  be the cause or what are the populations  or countries  with the largest decline so  unfortunately this study at any study is  limited to answer the question is there  a decline or not and what is the  magnitude however for my studies and  from other people studies we can say  something about your question so we know  many of the problems that harm sperm  count and harm male fertility we know  that male reproductive health is  determined mainly in two periods in life  in the fetal period when the main  reproductive system is developing and  this time you need a very specific  cascade of hormones in order for the  testicles to develop properly and we  know that even one part per million one  drop in a swimming pool of an endocrine  disruptor could disrupt this development  