  consequences so when it comes to human  reproduction as you mentioned we're  talking about the production of sperm  cells by the millions you only need one  in order to have a baby so conceivably  we could say that the impact is not  necessarily the greatest when it comes  to human reproduction because there's a  sort of significant buffer built in  because you produce millions if you  divide that by two it's still millions  you just need one but it seems as though  this issue is connected to many other  areas of health for men is that true or  do you believe that I'm wrong that it is  an issue for human reproduction  so you know as we say you're both right  and one so this is the storm claim and  the problem now it's only the tip of the  iceberg  the problem may continue if we don't  understand what are the causes  I'm not saying but other people saying  if you take the same graph by 2050 we'll  reach zero and so I don't want to  predict the future we don't know what  will happen in the future but even if  there is a chance of 1% that this  decline will continue and we'll you know  almost complete lack of sperm we  definitely need you know to do something  about it yeah at that point it will be a  problem for reproduction right also also  I know for my friends and maybe you and  I of us know from their friends that  nowadays many couples have problem  conceiving they take them longer time  and many of them need assisted  reproduction now you know the animals  while they don't use assisted  reproduction we and we think we are you  know the best animal in the world we are  unable to do the most basic function of  life to reproduce we need technology in  order to reproduce and the most common  fact oh when you study couples that have  problem conceiving the most common  problem is a male factor so I mean it's  40 percent coding you know different  studies but 40 percent male factor 30  percent female factor and 30 percent  combined or unknown factor so this is a  huge problem nowadays many men nowadays  are subfertile which is a bit under the  radar because we don't talk about it and  it's neglected we don't admit it and we  have technological solutions so the  problem is already now but besides that  my point that this is a very important  indicator it means that we have a  problem with our way of living that  seriously disrupt a major  function and if we don't understand what  are the causes and reverse them we will  continue to be insulted and this goes  together with increased in many  different kinds of cancer in specific  congenital malformations and in men  cells in general so as an indicator you  know we need to do something about it  now also about the first issue just to  say when you go before 40 million for ML  studies shows that you start to be  subverted now the mean got from 99 to 47  it means that a huge proportion is below  40  so clearly already now the percentage of  men that are sub fertile is definitely  larger than it used to be 50 years ago  so we need to do something about it  because of fertility issues because of  health issue and because it reflects a  problem that we need to acknowledge okay  in the very limited time we have left I  would love to hear from you I know you  said we don't know what is causing it  but can you give us some sense of what  some of the studies are looking at in  other words are we talking about diet  are we talking about pharmaceuticals are  we talking about environmental factors  are we talking about all of these things  so first we looked at sperm count but  maybe also the sperm function is  hampered maybe one sperm that belong to  a population that is smaller that what  used to be before  maybe is also damaged in some way and we  do have studies answering your question  we do have Studies on  endocrine disrupting chemicals which  hints that they not only empower and  impair the quantity but also the quality  we see epigenetic changes in the single  sperm so  personally I am studying and I'm worried  about the impact of pesticides and of  phthalates and other man-made chemicals  on our fetal development especially of  the male and on our you know on our  bodies during adult life and we are  doing a mass experiment on human  population exposing them for the first  time to many many chemicals that  interact together and maybe some of them  are very harmful not only to ourselves  but also also to next generations and  the effects may be transgenerational so  personally I am very worried about  chemicals and I think we should do much  more  not only in studying these chemicals but  also reversing the burden of proof I  think that before you introduce a  chemical to mass abuse and exposure of  the public it's your obligation to show  that is harmless nowadays you can use  any chemicals exposed many people and  unless you see the bodies and have a  clear proof that it's harmful there is  no regulation so that's my main worry  together with that I think that we can  do much better job in public health in  preventing obesity improving our diet  eating less processed food being more  physically active fighting tobacco and  there smoking all of these factors that  clearly harm our personal and public  health and also made fertility we've  been speaking with dr. Hagee Levine he  is at Hebrew University's Hadassah  School of Public Health thank you so  much for talking to us about this issue  today  thank you David it was a pleasure

